EXERCISE 51 — GETTING INPUT FROM A BROWSER

The book's exercise focuses on how a browser sends a request to a server and how HTML forms can be used to collect input.

Question

Create a webpage containing a form:

Name: [________]
[Submit]
Modern Flask answer
from flask import Flask, request

app = Flask(__name__)


@app.route("/", methods=["GET", "POST"])
def home():
    if request.method == "POST":
        name = request.form["name"]
        return f"Hello, {name}!"

    return """
    <form method="post">
        <input name="name">
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
    """


if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run()
EXERCISE 52 — THE START OF YOUR WEB GAME

This brings the game concepts into a web application. The book discusses refactoring the game, automated tests, map structure and game endings.

Mini Challenge

Create a web game:

You are in a room.

Choose:
1. Treasure
2. Monster
Answer
from flask import Flask, request

app = Flask(__name__)


@app.route("/", methods=["GET", "POST"])
def game():

    if request.method == "POST":

        choice = request.form["choice"]

        if choice == "1":
            return "<h1>You found the treasure! YOU WIN!</h1>"

        elif choice == "2":
            return "<h1>A monster attacked! GAME OVER!</h1>"

        else:
            return "<h1>Invalid choice</h1>"

    return """
    <h1>Welcome to the Game</h1>

    <form method="post">

        <p>Choose your path:</p>

        <input type="radio" name="choice" value="1">
        Treasure

        <br>

        <input type="radio" name="choice" value="2">
        Monster

        <br><br>

        <button type="submit">Choose</button>

    </form>
    """


if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run()
